o then  helping us players to experience the  state is pretty much the goal of every  video game developer even if they might  not know it okay let's get tentacle so  what does a task or in our case a game  need to do to get players experiencing  cognitive flow well there are three  things broadly speaking required to get  a player in the zone the game must have  clear goals and direction in other words  you must know what it is that you have  to do or can do next  now I'm sure we've all experienced  moments of the opposite of playing game  where you just have no idea where you're  meant to be going next this can be  frustrating and importantly flow  breaking  the task must have feedback a crucial  part of maintaining flow in gaming is  being told by the game that you're doing  a good job or a bad job just feedback  that our actions actually matter and are  taking an effect on the world the player  must have confidence that they are  capable of completing the task so the  level of challenge must be appropriate  okay now that last one that balance  between challenge or difficulty level  and skill level is absolutely crucial in  attaining a flow state if a task is too  simple then the player can easily become  bored or apathetic and lose the state of  flow on the other hand if it's too  difficult it can make the player anxious  and frustrated again breaking flow check  out this picture chart now as it  demonstrates flow is at its peak of the  apex of high difficulty and high skill  so a game which trains you well on the  mechanics while deftly increasing the  difficulty level to match should be the  ultimate initiator of flow interestingly  not everyone can experience cognitive  flow with the same level of ease now  this could have something to do with a  separate psychological idea known as the  locus of control this refers to the  extent that an individual believes that  they are in control of events that  affect them  so applied to a video game situation  it's the 